---
/**
 * CtaBand — the block that closes a page.
 *
 * The ground runs brand-500 into brand-900, top to bottom, with a grid over
 * it. One hue, so it gives depth rather than a colour change.
 *
 * It is not the home hero's gradient. That one runs brand-500 to black, and
 * black has no chroma: across 62 points of lightness a band carrying it has a
 * dead end. The hero survives that because it is a screen tall and the black
 * end sits below the fold, so the two ends are never on screen together. A
 * band a few hundred pixels tall shows the whole ramp at once and reads as a
 * hole. brand-900 keeps the band in the brand's own hue at the bottom and
 * still leaves it somewhere to travel.
 *
 * Top to bottom rather than on a diagonal. A diagonal puts the light end in
 * one corner, which on a band this wide reads as a corner that has been lit.
 * Vertical is also what lets the light stop be the brand at all: the heading
 * sits near the top where the ground is lightest and is large and bold, which
 * WCAG scores at 3:1, while the description sits lower on darker ground where
 * it has the 4.5 it needs.
 *
 * One ground in both colour modes, on purpose. `.invert-section` remaps the
 * foreground tokens underneath, so the colour mode shows in the page around
 * the band rather than inside it.
 *
 * Nothing sits between the content and the gradient. The LetterGlitch band
 * this replaces carried an inner glass panel, which was there to hold text off
 * a moving canvas; nothing moves here, and two nested rounded boxes read busy.
 *
 * No border. The LetterGlitch band was near-black and needed an edge to
 * separate it from a dark page. A saturated field defines its own edge.
 *
 * No pointer or width gate. LetterGlitch is a canvas effect, so it ran only
 * above `glitch` (1024px and a fine pointer) and every screen below that got a
 * second, flat CTA block saying the same thing. A gradient costs nothing, so
 * one band serves every width and the duplicate is gone.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Max width of the band wrapper. Defaults to 6xl, matching the section
   *  containers; surfaces running full-bleed content above their CTA pass
   *  7xl, as they did to the band this replaces. */
  maxWidth?: '6xl' | '7xl';
  /** Draw the grid. On by default.
   *
   *  Turn it off behind see-through content: the grid's lines read straight
   *  through a translucent pill and a line crossing one cuts it in half. A
   *  band closing on an opaque button has nothing to show through. */
  grid?: boolean;
  /** Extra classes for the outer wrapper. */
  class?: string;
}

const { maxWidth = '6xl', grid = true, class: className } = Astro.props;
const widthClass = maxWidth === '7xl' ? 'max-w-7xl' : 'max-w-6xl';
---

<div class:list={['mx-auto px-6', widthClass, className]}>
  <div class="relative isolate">
    <div class="cta-band invert-section relative overflow-hidden rounded-3xl">
      {grid && <div class="cta-grid absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true" />}
      <div class="relative z-10 px-6 py-20 sm:py-32" data-reveal>
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
          <slot />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* The light stop sits at -16%, outside the box, so the visible band starts
     about an eighth of the way down the ramp rather than at full brand-500.

     A legibility fix, and one that only surfaces on the narrow case. A phone
     renders the band short with the content nearly filling it, so the
     description sits in the brightest part of the gradient, where a desktop's
     taller band puts it in the middle. At 0% the worst case fell under the
     4.5 WCAG asks of body text.

     -16% rather than further: on one hue the band has only lightness to work
     with, so the brightness at the top is what keeps it from reading as a flat
     slab, and every percent of head start spends some of it. */
  .cta-band {
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      var(--color-brand-500) -16%,
      var(--color-brand-900) 100%
    );
  }

  /* The grid — 60px squares, radial-masked so the lines fade out before they
     reach the corners rather than stopping at them.

     White at 9% rather than a brand tint: brand-500 IS the top stop here, so a
     brand-coloured line is invisible against it. White holds across the whole
     travel from brand-500 down to brand-900, and does so in every palette
     because it carries no hue of its own.

     1px lines on a 60px square, so the ground under any text row barely
     moves. */
  .cta-grid {
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(to right, rgb(255 255 255 / 0.09) 1px, transparent 1px),
      linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(255 255 255 / 0.09) 1px, transparent 1px);
    background-size: 60px 60px;
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, #000 45%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, #000 45%, transparent 100%);
  }

  /* The band is a saturated field, so the theme's shadow tiers — a low-opacity
     grey wash — cannot register against its edge. Light mode gets a heavy drop
     shadow, which is where it earns its keep: the band sits on a near-white
     page and the shadow is what lifts it off.

     Dark mode gets nothing. A glow was inherited from the LetterGlitch band,
     on the reasoning that a dark shadow cannot show on a dark page but a glow
     can. It can, and that is the objection — a lit halo around a card reads as
     an effect rather than as depth. On a near-black page the band's own edge
     is the contrast. */
  .cta-band {
    box-shadow:
      0 32px 64px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.55),
      0 0 32px -4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.25);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .cta-band {
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  /* Body copy on the gradient. `invert-section` resolves
     `.text-foreground-muted` to `--on-invert-muted`, which is pitched for a
     near-black section and goes dim on a saturated field.

     Lifted to 89%, and tinted from the active brand hue rather than a fixed
     one, so the lift lands the same way in every palette. The token is
     the fallback where relative colour syntax is missing — hue-correct but
     lighter, so the text stays readable there too. This mirrors what
     `.hero-dark-gradient` does for the same reason. */
  .cta-band :global(.text-foreground-muted) {
    color: var(--on-invert-secondary);
  }
  @supports (color: oklch(from red 50% 0.1 h)) {
    .cta-band :global(.text-foreground-muted) {
      color: oklch(from var(--color-brand-500) 89% 0.012 h);
    }
  }

  /* The content column is max-w-2xl — 672px — which a large heading wants for
     its own line breaks. The description does not: one line running the width
     of the band under a heading half its length leaves nothing overflowing for
     `text-balance` to work on. 34rem holds it to a two-line block. */
  .cta-band :global(p) {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
